{
  "dom_id": "step-q94154",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\u03a6(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z\u223cN(0,1),$ compute $P(Z\\ge 2.11).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9861\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "We're given that $Z\u223cN(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z\\ge 2.11).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/1d2d207ca7c72b0e4ee5a8b34c0f48f288a104de643ab552ccbfec15dbd08fcf.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\u03a6(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=\u03a6(z).\n$$\n\nSince the total area under the graph equals $1,$ we can express the required probability in terms of $\u03a6(z)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 2.11) & =1-P(Z<2.11) \\\\  & =1-\u03a6(2.11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/0e0d5909a42ae2ee25dbee577b68fa0c6aef45285b6157f610d5629b227c2752.png)\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\u03a6(2.11)=0.9826.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 2.11) & =1-\u03a6(2.11) \\\\  & =1-0.9826 \\\\  & =0.0174.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
